# CHANGELOG.md is append-only: every PR adds an entry at the same spot under
# [Unreleased], so the first merge forces a rebase on every other open PR.
# The union driver keeps both sides' entries instead of conflicting.
# Deliberately scoped to this one file — union resolves by concatenation with no
# conflict, which is correct for a changelog and wrong for structured files.
#
# The driver is LOAD-BEARING. This repo's workflow merges origin/main into each
# branch LOCALLY before the PR lands, and local git honours the driver. Measured
# 2026-09-02 at main 8ab4618 across all 50 open PRs targeting main, replayed at
# that same main: 15 are clean with union and conflict without it, and in all 15
# the ONLY conflicting file is CHANGELOG.md. Do not delete this line and do not
# migrate to changelog fragments.
#
# CAVEAT — github.com will still show "This branch has conflicts", because
# GitHub's SERVER DOES NOT APPLY THIS DRIVER. Merge drivers are a local-checkout
# feature; GitHub computes PR mergeability with the default text driver. So a PR
# that touches [Unreleased] flips to CONFLICTING as soon as another such PR
# merges ahead of it. The driver makes the fix free; it does not prevent the flag.
#
# That is measured, not assumed — 2026-09-02 (WIC-1904), and it settles a
# question this file has now answered three different ways. Two designs, same
# answer:
#
#   * CROSS-SECTION. All 50 open main-targeting PRs, each force-polled for a
#     fresh `mergeable` and replayed at that same main 8ab4618 both with and
#     without union. The flag matches the NO-UNION replay 50 of 50 (32
#     MERGEABLE/clean, 18 CONFLICTING/conflict). The WITH-union replay disagrees
#     on exactly 15 — the union-dependent set. There is no coincidence available:
#     the two replays differ on 15 PRs and GitHub sides with no-union on all 15.
#
#   * PAIRED TRANSITION, which controls for everything PR-specific. Five PRs
#     (#227 #318 #335 #337 #342) whose heads did not change across a main move
#     7f8646d -> 8ab4618. At the old base all five were clean without union and
#     all five read MERGEABLE. At the new base exactly three conflict without
#     union — and exactly those three flipped to CONFLICTING. Same PR, same head,
#     only the base moved.
#
# So this file's ORIGINAL claim was right, and its 2026-09-01 withdrawal of it
# (which said the question was unmeasured in both directions) is itself
# withdrawn. Two earlier readings failed for two different reasons, and telling
# them apart is the whole lesson:
#
#   1. STALE READS, not a sticky-dirty conflict. `mergeable` is not recomputed
#      on demand, and the LIST endpoint (`gh pr list --json mergeable`) hands
#      back UNKNOWN or a stale value — on this sweep it returned UNKNOWN for
#      most of the queue while GitHub was recomputing. Poll each PR
#      individually and RETRY UNTIL IT IS NOT UNKNOWN. Every "the flag says
#      CONFLICTING but it merges clean" result here came from a read taken at a
#      base GitHub had already moved off. Once freshly resolved, the flag was
#      exact on all 50.
#
#   2. A SELECTION ARTIFACT that could only ever return "no evidence". The
#      previous revision looked for the discriminating case — a PR GitHub
#      computes clean while it genuinely conflicts without union at that same
#      base — and required it to have a FRESH refs/pull/N/merge. That test is
#      incapable of finding anything: GitHub only writes the merge ref when the
#      merge SUCCEEDS, so a conflicting PR ALWAYS has a stale or absent ref.
#      Measured today, 18 of 18 CONFLICTING PRs have a stale (17) or missing (1)
#      ref, while 26 of 32 MERGEABLE PRs ALSO read stale. Ref freshness is a
#      one-sided signal: FRESH proves GitHub computed a clean merge, but STALE
#      proves nothing at all. Requiring a fresh ref on a conflicting PR is
#      requiring a contradiction, which is why the case looked "structurally
#      empty" when in fact it had 15 members that day.
#
# Earlier figures in this file (WIC-1363, 2026-08-26: "of 30 open PRs, 25
# touched CHANGELOG.md and 17 read CONFLICTING") counted unrefreshed flags
# rather than conflicts, and are superseded by the sweep above.
#
# To decide whether a PR really is mergeable, use ground truth, never a single
# read of the flag: fetch refs/pull/N/merge and check it is a 2-parent commit
# with no conflict markers AND that its first parent is the current base tip
# (FRESH certifies clean; stale certifies nothing — see 2 above), or run a
# real merge at the PR's current base in a throwaway worktree. If you use
# `git merge-tree`, --attr-source is a GLOBAL option and must come before the
# subcommand (`git --attr-source=<base> merge-tree ...`); placed after it, git
# exits 129, and omitted entirely it reads attributes from the working tree and
# measures nothing.
#
# So: do not go looking for a real conflict, and do not rebase a PR that others
# are stacked on. Merge main in and push — the driver resolves it on the way
# through, and the merge commit is what flips GitHub back to mergeable:
#
#     git fetch origin && git merge origin/main && git push
#
# If that leaves conflict markers in CHANGELOG.md, the driver did not fire (the
# file is only union-merged when .gitattributes is present in the checkout) and
# you are resolving by hand. Keep BOTH entries, newest date first; the sides are
# additive and neither edits the other's text.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
